amig=f'''
<section class="page-hero lavender-hero"><div class="shell page-hero-layout"><div><span class="eyebrow">✦ THE ART OF LITTLE CREATURES</span><h1>Meet the magic of <em>amigurumi.</em></h1><p>Small stitches, big personalities. Get inspired, choose your supplies, and crochet a pocket-size friend.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="#citrus-pattern">Try the free pattern ↓</a></div>{figure('turtle','Small crocheted turtle amigurumi figures','','page-hero-photo')}</div></section>
<section class="section shell"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="overline">STUFFED WITH IMAGINATION</span><h2>Three things to love about amigurumi</h2></div></div><div class="article-stack">
<article class="feature-article"><div class="feature-text"><span class="number-label">01 / THE BASICS</span><h3>What is amigurumi?</h3><p>Amigurumi is the Japanese art of making small stuffed figures using crochet or knitting. Modern crocheted amigurumi often uses single crochet worked in continuous spirals, which creates a firm fabric ideal for shaping bears, bunnies, fruit, and fantasy creatures.</p><p>The secret is surprisingly simple: increases make a circle grow, straight rounds add height, and decreases close a shape. Add stuffing and an embroidered expression, and your little character comes to life.</p><div class="tip-note">Start with a round character that has very few pieces to sew together.</div></div>{two_photos('bear','cat','Crocheted red amigurumi bear','Amigurumi black cat character')}</article>
<article class="feature-article reverse"><div class="feature-text"><span class="number-label">02 / MATERIALS</span><h3>Choosing the right supplies</h3><p>Smooth cotton or acrylic yarn makes stitches easier to see and count. Worsted-weight yarn with a hook a little smaller than the yarn label suggests is often a friendly starting point. A stitch marker keeps track of round beginnings while a tapestry needle helps join pieces neatly.</p><p>Stuff slowly with small tufts of polyester fill to avoid lumpy shapes. For toys intended for babies or pets, embroider eyes and skip safety eyes, buttons, bells, beads, and any other detachable parts.</p><div class="tip-note">A tight fabric keeps stuffing inside and gives your figure a clean shape.</div></div>{two_photos('unicorn','chicks','Hand-crocheted unicorn with colorful mane','Small crocheted chick toys')}</article>
<article class="feature-article"><div class="feature-text"><span class="number-label">03 / PERSONALITY</span><h3>Shape, color &amp; expression</h3><p>Amigurumi is a playful lesson in construction. Start with a sphere or oval, build ears or limbs separately, and pin the parts in place before stitching them on. Small changes in the eyes, mouth, and placement of the ears can completely change a toy's personality.</p><p>Try a limited color palette, count stitches at the end of each round, and use invisible decreases when working through both loops. After your first figure, change the colors or give it tiny accessories to make it yours.</p><div class="tip-note">Make both limbs before attaching them so you can compare their sizes.</div></div>{two_photos('turtle','octopus','Crocheted amigurumi turtle figures','Smiling yellow crocheted octopus')}</article>
</div></section>
<section class="pattern-section" id="citrus-pattern"><div class="shell"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="overline">FREE BEGINNER PATTERN</span><h2>Sunny Little Tangerine <span class="small-title">🍊</span></h2><p>A cheerful, palm-size amigurumi fruit, worked in continuous rounds.</p></div><button class="button button-outline print-button" type="button">Print this page ↗</button></div><div class="pattern-layout"><div class="pattern-copy"><div class="pattern-summary">{chips(['Beginner','US terms','Approx. 3 in / 7.5 cm','About 1–2 hours'])}</div><h3>You'll need</h3><ul class="pretty-list"><li>Orange worsted-weight yarn, about 30–45 yd</li><li>Small amount of green yarn for a leaf and stem</li><li>3.5 mm (E/4) crochet hook or size giving a firm fabric</li><li>Polyester stuffing, stitch marker, tapestry needle, scissors</li><li>Black embroidery floss for optional sleepy eyes</li></ul><h3>Stitch key</h3><p><strong>MR</strong> magic ring · <strong>sc</strong> single crochet · <strong>inc</strong> 2 sc in one stitch · <strong>dec</strong> invisible decrease · <strong>sl st</strong> slip stitch · <strong>ch</strong> chain. Numbers in parentheses are the stitch count at the end of the round.</p><h3>Body — orange yarn</h3><ol class="steps"><li>R1: 6 sc into MR. (6)</li><li>R2: Inc in each stitch around. (12)</li><li>R3: [1 sc, inc] × 6. (18)</li><li>R4: [2 sc, inc] × 6. (24)</li><li>R5: [3 sc, inc] × 6. (30)</li><li>R6: [4 sc, inc] × 6. (36)</li><li>R7–R12: Sc around for 6 rounds. (36 each)</li><li>R13: [4 sc, dec] × 6. (30)</li><li>R14: [3 sc, dec] × 6. (24)</li><li>R15: [2 sc, dec] × 6. (18) Stuff firmly but gently.</li><li>R16: [1 sc, dec] × 6. (12)</li><li>R17: Dec × 6. (6) Fasten off, thread through front loops of remaining stitches and pull closed.</li></ol><h3>Leaf &amp; finishing</h3><ol class="steps"><li>Leaf: With green yarn, ch 6. In the second ch from hook, sl st, then sc, hdc, sc, sl st along the chain. Fasten off, leaving a tail.</li><li>Stem: Ch 4 with green yarn, sl st back along the chain. Sew the stem and leaf over the body's closing point.</li><li>Optional face: Embroider two small curved eyes before final stuffing, keeping them on the same round.</li><li>Gently shape the fruit with your hands and weave in all ends securely.</li></ol><p class="pattern-disclaimer">Original beginner pattern by All Things Crochet. The freely licensed fruit photographs show a similar crocheted tangerine made by another artist; yours may look different.</p></div><aside class="pattern-side">{figure('tangerine_e','Finished crocheted orange amigurumi tangerine photographed on a tabletop','Finished tangerine inspiration')}{figure('tangerine_g','Second view of a crocheted tangerine amigurumi','Another view of the finished fruit')}<div class="pattern-hint"><span>✿ MAKER TIP</span><p>Use a removable stitch marker in the first stitch of each new round. Move it up as you go so your stitch counts stay on track.</p></div></aside></div></div></section>'''
page('amigurumi','Amigurumi','Discover the art of amigurumi with three illustrated articles and a free written tangerine amigurumi pattern.',amig,['turtle','bear','cat','unicorn','chicks','octopus','tangerine_e','tangerine_g'])

tips=f'''
<section class="page-hero blue-hero"><div class="shell page-hero-layout"><div><span class="eyebrow">✦ LITTLE TRICKS, LOVELY RESULTS</span><h1>Good tips. <em>Great stitches.</em></h1><p>Helpful habits for happier hook time, cuter creations, and the right yarn for every project.</p><a class="button button-primary" href="#yarn-guide">See the yarn guide ↓</a></div>{figure('granny','Close-up view of crochet granny square stitches','','page-hero-photo')}</div></section>
<section class="section shell tips-list"><article class="tip-feature"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="overline">01 · AMIGURUMI TIPS</span><h2>Tiny stitches, tidy toys</h2></div></div><div class="tip-grid"><div class="long-copy"><p>Amigurumi is easier when each little detail has a job. A smooth, light-colored yarn helps you see your stitches, while a hook smaller than the label's usual suggestion creates a dense fabric that holds stuffing inside.</p><ul class="check-list"><li>Mark the first stitch of every round and count at the end of each one.</li><li>Use invisible decreases to help keep the fabric smooth.</li><li>Stuff in small amounts; stop before the stitches stretch apart.</li><li>Pin ears, arms, or facial features in place before sewing them down.</li><li>For babies and pets, embroider eyes and avoid detachable parts.</li></ul></div>{two_photos('baby_octopus','tangerine_b','Crocheted octopus showing tight spiral construction','Small handmade tangerine amigurumi showing even stitches')}</div></article>
<article class="tip-feature"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="overline">02 · CROCHET TIPS &amp; TRICKS</span><h2>Make every row a little easier</h2></div></div><div class="tip-grid"><div class="long-copy"><p>Even experienced crocheters return to the basics. Before starting a big project, create a small gauge swatch and compare it with your pattern. A consistent stitch height is often more important than trying to crochet quickly.</p><ul class="check-list"><li>Use the same hook and yarn tension throughout your project.</li><li>Keep a small notebook for hook sizes, gauge, and pattern adjustments.</li><li>Count turning chains correctly: a pattern will tell you whether they count as a stitch.</li><li>Pull out a few stitches to correct an early mistake rather than hiding it later.</li><li>Weave in ends in several directions for a more secure finish.</li></ul></div><div class="single-photo">{figure('stitch','Close-up demonstration of modified single crochet stitch','Practice neat, even stitches')}</div></div></article>
<article class="tip-feature" id="yarn-guide"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="overline">03 · YARN GUIDE</span><h2>Yarn fibers, weights &amp; what they're for</h2><p>Two things matter most when choosing yarn: what it's made of and how thick it is.</p></div></div><div class="yarn-gallery">{figure('skeins','Assorted bright skeins of colorful yarn','Color & texture')}{figure('dyelot','Matching yarn skeins from the same dye lot','Matching dye lots')}{figure('yarnball','Thick crocheted soft yarn ball','Bulky yarn inspiration')}</div><div class="long-copy yarn-intro"><p><strong>Cotton</strong> offers crisp stitch definition and is handy for baskets, amigurumi, and summer accessories. <strong>Acrylic</strong> is affordable, colorful, and often easy to care for, making it a popular choice for blankets and toys. <strong>Wool</strong> brings warmth and elasticity for hats, sweaters, and winter pieces. <strong>Bamboo, linen, and blends</strong> can offer drape or breathability for garments and shawls. Always follow the yarn's care label.</p><p>Yarn weights are standardized categories, not strict measurements. The table is a starting guide; check the specific yarn label and your pattern for hook size and gauge.</p></div><div class="table-wrap"><table class="yarn-table"><thead><tr><th>Weight</th><th>Common name</th><th>Popular uses</th><th>Typical hook*</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td><span class="weight-circle">0</span> Lace</td><td>Lace, crochet thread</td><td>Fine lace, delicate shawls, doilies</td><td>1.5–2.25 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="weight-circle">1</span> Super Fine</td><td>Fingering, sock</td><td>Light shawls, baby items, fine wearables</td><td>2.25–3.5 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="weight-circle">2</span> Fine</td><td>Sport, baby</td><td>Light sweaters, toys, soft baby blankets</td><td>3.5–4.5 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="weight-circle">3</span> Light</td><td>DK, light worsted</td><td>Garments, hats, soft toys, blankets</td><td>4.5–5.5 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="weight-circle">4</span> Medium</td><td>Worsted, aran</td><td>Amigurumi, scarves, throws, beginner projects</td><td>5.5–6.5 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="weight-circle">5</span> Bulky</td><td>Chunky</td><td>Quick hats, cozy scarves, pillows</td><td>6.5–9 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="weight-circle">6</span> Super Bulky</td><td>Super chunky</td><td>Fast blankets, plush accessories, baskets</td><td>9–15 mm</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="weight-circle">7</span> Jumbo</td><td>Jumbo, roving-style</td><td>Very chunky blankets and statement decor</td><td>15 mm and up</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div><p class="micro-note">*Approximate crochet-hook ranges for general guidance, not universal standards. For amigurumi, a smaller hook is often used to form a firm fabric.</p></article></section>'''
page('tips','Tips','Three detailed illustrated crochet tips articles, including amigurumi techniques and a complete yarn fibers and weights reference.',tips,['granny','baby_octopus','tangerine_b','stitch','skeins','dyelot','yarnball'])

patterns=f'''
<section class="page-hero lavender-hero pattern-hero"><div class="shell page-hero-layout"><div><span class="eyebrow">✦ PICK A PROJECT &amp; GET HOOKING</span><h1>Patterns &amp; <em>helpful tools.</em></h1><p>A little planning goes a long way. Estimate your yarn, then turn it into something soft, sweet, and handmade.</p><a href="#yardage-calculator" class="button button-primary">Try the calculator ↓</a></div>{figure('squarescarf','Crocheted hexagon motifs forming a handmade scarf','','page-hero-photo')}</div></section>
<section class="section shell utility-section" id="yardage-calculator"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="overline">YOUR NEW FAVORITE TOOL</span><h2>Interactive yardage calculator</h2><p>Use a small swatch to estimate how much yarn your project may need.</p></div></div><div class="calculator-layout"><form id="yardage-form" class="calculator-card" novalidate><div class="calc-title">🧶 <h3>Let's do the yarn math</h3></div><p class="calc-help">Make a gauge swatch using the stitch and yarn for your project, then weigh it. Enter measurements in inches and weight in grams.</p><fieldset><legend>01 · Your swatch</legend><div class="form-grid"><label>Width (in)<input name="swatchWidth" type="number" min="0.1" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" value="4" required></label><label>Height (in)<input name="swatchHeight" type="number" min="0.1" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" value="4" required></label><label>Swatch weight (g)<input name="swatchWeight" type="number" min="0.1" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" value="6" required></label></div></fieldset><fieldset><legend>02 · Finished project size</legend><div class="form-grid"><label>Width (in)<input name="projectWidth" type="number" min="0.1" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" value="7" required></label><label>Length (in)<input name="projectHeight" type="number" min="0.1" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" value="60" required></label></div></fieldset><fieldset><legend>03 · Your yarn label</legend><div class="form-grid"><label>Yards per skein<input name="skeinYards" type="number" min="0.1" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" value="220" required></label><label>Grams per skein<input name="skeinGrams" type="number" min="0.1" step="0.1" inputmode="decimal" value="100" required></label><label>Extra allowance (%)<input name="buffer" type="number" min="0" max="200" step="1" value="15" required></label></div></fieldset><div class="button-row"><button type="submit" class="button button-primary">Calculate yardage →</button><button type="reset" class="button button-outline">Reset</button></div></form><div class="calc-results"><span class="overline">YOUR ESTIMATE</span><div class="result-box" aria-live="polite"><div class="result-label">Estimated yarn needed</div><div class="result-value"><span id="result-yards">—</span> <small>yards</small></div><div class="result-row"><span>Approximate yarn weight</span><strong id="result-grams">—</strong></div><div class="result-row"><span>Whole skeins to buy</span><strong id="result-skeins">—</strong></div><p id="calculator-message" class="calc-message">Enter your measurements and calculate to see your estimate.</p></div><div class="calc-explain"><h3>How the estimate works</h3><p>We scale the swatch's weight by the area of your project, then use the yarn label's yards-per-gram ratio. Your extra allowance is included in the result.</p><p><strong>Keep in mind:</strong> stitch patterns, fringe, shaping, gauge changes, and three-dimensional pieces can change actual yarn use. This tool is best for flat projects, like scarves and blankets.</p></div></div></div></section>
<section class="pattern-section alternate" id="pet-pattern"><div class="shell"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="overline">FREE PATTERN · PETS</span><h2>Little Kitty Crochet Ball</h2><p>A small, soft ball for supervised play — no plastic eyes, buttons, or bells.</p></div><button class="button button-outline print-button" type="button">Print this page ↗</button></div><div class="pattern-layout"><div class="pattern-copy">{chips(['Easy','US crochet terms','Approx. 2.5 in / 6 cm','About 30–60 min'])}<h3>Supplies</h3><ul class="pretty-list"><li>Approx. 20–30 yards of sturdy washable worsted cotton yarn</li><li>3.5 mm hook (or size creating a tight fabric), stitch marker</li><li>Tapestry needle; small amount of fiberfill or washable fabric scraps</li></ul><h3>Stitches</h3><p>MR = magic ring · sc = single crochet · inc = 2 sc in same stitch · dec = invisible decrease.</p><h3>Instructions</h3><ol class="steps"><li>R1: 6 sc into MR. (6)</li><li>R2: Inc in every stitch. (12)</li><li>R3: [1 sc, inc] × 6. (18)</li><li>R4: [2 sc, inc] × 6. (24)</li><li>R5–R9: Sc in each stitch around. (24 each)</li><li>R10: [2 sc, dec] × 6. (18)</li><li>R11: [1 sc, dec] × 6. (12) Lightly stuff now.</li><li>R12: Dec × 6. (6) Fasten off with a long tail. Thread through the remaining front loops, pull closed, then bury and secure the tail inside.</li></ol><h3>Finishing &amp; safety</h3><p>Inspect every seam and weave in ends firmly. <strong>Always supervise pets</strong> and remove the toy immediately if it frays or opens. Some pets swallow string or stuffing; never leave yarn toys unattended, and skip this project for pets that chew and ingest fabric. Wash and dry it thoroughly as needed.</p><p class="pattern-disclaimer">Original pattern by All Things Crochet. Photos are illustrative examples of other hand-crocheted pet-friendly toy ideas, not an exact sample of this ball.</p></div><aside class="pattern-side">{figure('yarnball','Soft crocheted yarn ball used as a cat toy','Crocheted ball inspiration')}{figure('mouse','Crocheted gray mouse cat toy showing another pet-toy idea','More pet-toy inspiration')}<div class="pattern-hint"><span>♡ PET-SAFE DETAILS</span><p>Simple, embroidered-free designs with secure seams are safer than toys with detachable decorations — but supervision is always essential.</p></div></aside></div></div></section>
<section class="pattern-section" id="scarf-pattern"><div class="shell"><div class="section-heading"><div><span class="overline">FREE PATTERN · ACCESSORIES</span><h2>Cozy Weekend Scarf</h2><p>A soft, textured rectangle that is easy to customize for any length.</p></div><button class="button button-outline print-button" type="button">Print this page ↗</button></div><div class="pattern-layout"><div class="pattern-copy">{chips(['Confident beginner','US crochet terms','Approx. 7 × 60 in','3–5 hours'])}<h3>Supplies</h3><ul class="pretty-list"><li>Approx. 350–425 yards of medium (weight 4) yarn</li><li>5.5 mm (I/9) crochet hook, or a hook that achieves your preferred drape</li><li>Tapestry needle, scissors, measuring tape</li></ul><h3>Stitches</h3><p>ch = chain · hdc = half double crochet · BLO = back loop only · st = stitch. A turning chain does not count as a stitch in this pattern.</p><h3>Instructions</h3><ol class="steps"><li>Foundation: Ch 24 loosely.</li><li>Row 1: Hdc in 3rd ch from hook and in each ch across. (22 hdc)</li><li>Row 2: Ch 2, turn. Hdc in BLO of each st across. (22 hdc)</li><li>Rows 3 onward: Repeat Row 2 until the scarf measures approximately 60 in (152 cm), or your chosen length.</li><li>Last row: Ch 1, fasten off. Weave in all yarn ends securely.</li><li>Optional fringe: Cut 10–12 in strands, fold them in groups, and loop them evenly through each short end. Trim to match.</li></ol><h3>Make it yours</h3><p>For a wider scarf, add chains in the foundation; each extra chain adds one hdc to every row. For a longer scarf, repeat Row 2. Check the drape after a few rows, and use the yardage calculator above to estimate yarn for your chosen dimensions.</p><p class="pattern-disclaimer">Original scarf pattern by All Things Crochet. Photos show other crocheted scarves for design inspiration, rather than the exact pattern sample.</p></div><aside class="pattern-side">{figure('scarf','Finished crocheted scarf using textured yarn and a shell border','Crochet scarf inspiration')}{figure('squarescarf','Crocheted scarf made from hexagon motifs','A second scarf style to inspire you')}<div class="pattern-hint"><span>✳ NO-STRESS TIP</span><p>Measure your first few rows before committing to the full scarf. A small gauge change can make a big difference in the final width.</p></div></aside></div></div></section>'''
page('patterns','Patterns & Tools','Two free complete crochet patterns for a pet toy and scarf, plus an interactive crochet yardage calculator.',patterns,['squarescarf','yarnball','mouse','scarf'])
